diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8f85ba8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,225 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots are members of rooms that post messages over HTTP. This note records how they
+are built and why, for whoever extends them next.
+
+## Model
+
+A bot is a `User` with `role: :bot` (`User::Role`), not a separate model. Because
+of that, bots get everything people get for free: room memberships (they appear in
+the closed-room member picker; see "Rooms a bot is in" for open rooms), avatars,
+message authorship, mentions, broadcasts and push notifications to other members.
+
+`User::Bot` adds:
+
+- `creator` – who added the bot (`users.creator_id`). This also makes
+  `Current.user.can_administer?(bot)` work like it does for rooms and messages.
+- `bot_token` – random secret; the **bot code** shown in the UI is `bot_key`,
+  i.e. `"<id>-<token>"`.
+- `User.authenticate_bot(bot_key)` – looks the bot up by id among *active* bots
+  and compares the token with `secure_compare`.
+- `manageable_bots` – your own bots, or all bots for administrators. Every
+  `/bots` controller looks bots up through this, so others' bots are a 404.
+- `deactivate_bot` – "Delete this chat bot". It deactivates the user (keeping its
+  messages and authorship) and clears the token, so the old code stops working.
+- `reset_bot_key` – rotate a leaked code (`PATCH /bots/:bot_id/key`).
+
+Bots have no email address or password (`users.password_digest` is nullable; a
+validation still requires it for people), so they can't sign in or get a session.
+Transfer links and `/account/users` exclude bots (`User.without_bots`), so a bot
+can't be turned into a human administrator or handed a session.
+
+## Rooms a bot is in
+
+People join every open room; bots don't, unless their creator turns on **"Add to all
+open rooms"** on the bot form (`users.joins_open_rooms`, off by default).
+
+- `User.joining_open_rooms` = people + bots with the switch on. `Rooms::Open` grants
+  `User.active.joining_open_rooms` when a room is created or converted to open.
+  Bots already in a closed room that becomes open stay in it.
+- `User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms` runs `after_save_commit` if
+  `joining_open_rooms?`: for people on creation; for (active) bots whenever
+  `joins_open_rooms` changes to `true` (on creation or later on edit).
+  It's a single `after_save_commit` on purpose: registering the same method in both
+  `after_create_commit` and `after_update_commit` makes Rails keep only the last one.
+- Turning the switch off changes nothing about current memberships.
+
+## The `joined` event
+
+Every membership goes through `Membership.grant` (`Membership::Grantable`), which
+both `Room#memberships.grant_to` and `User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms` use. It
+`insert_all`s with `returning: :id`; SQLite's `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` only returns
+rows that were actually inserted, so memberships that already existed (re-saving a
+closed room, toggling the switch off and on, concurrent grants) never produce a second
+event. For the new memberships of active bots with a webhook, in non-direct rooms, it
+enqueues `User::DeliverJoinedWebhookJob(bot, room)`.
+
+That job sets `enqueue_after_transaction_commit = true` (the app default is `false`)
+because grants often happen inside `Room.create_for`'s transaction.
+
+Payload: `{ event: "joined", room: { id, name, reply_to } }` (no author/message).
+Replies are handled exactly like `mention` replies and posted into that room.
+
+A bot removed from a room and added again is a new membership, so it gets `joined`
+again. Direct rooms never trigger it.
+
+## Posting: `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages`
+
+`Messages::ByBotsController`, authenticated with `allow_bot_access` (in the
+`Authentication` concern: skips the session check and CSRF, sets `Current.user`
+to the bot).
+
+- `401` bad/unknown/deleted bot code (checked before the room).
+- `404` room doesn't exist or the bot isn't a member of it.
+- `422` empty (or whitespace-only) body and no attachment.
+- `201` with `Location: /messages/:id`.
+
+The raw request body *is* the message. `curl -d` sends
+`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, and Rails would try to parse e.g.
+`100% done` as form params (raising `BadRequest`) during instrumentation, before
+any callback runs. So the controller overrides `process_action` to skip param
+parsing unless the request is `multipart/form-data` (used for
+`curl -F attachment=@file`). Route params (`room_id`, `bot_key`) come from the
+path and are unaffected.
+
+The body is treated as plain text: it's HTML-escaped and newlines become `<br>`
+before going into the rich text body, so `Deploy <v1.2>` shows as written.
+
+After saving, it does what `MessagesController#create` does: process the
+attachment and `broadcast_create`; `Room#receive` (after commit) marks rooms
+unread and enqueues push notifications.
+
+## Keeping the bot code secret
+
+- Shown only on `/bots`, to its creator and administrators.
+- `bot_key` params are filtered from logs by the existing `:_key` filter.
+- The code is in the URL path too, so `lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb`
+  masks it in `request.filtered_path` (the `Started POST ...` log line and
+  instrumentation payloads). Upstream proxies may still log full paths.
+
+## Webhooks: talking *to* a bot
+
+A bot may have an optional `users.webhook_url` ("Webhook URL" on the bot form).
+It's normalized (`strip.presence`), so saving the form with an empty field sets it
+to `nil` and removes the webhook. Only `http://` / `https://` URLs with a host are
+valid. Private/loopback addresses are deliberately *not* blocked (unlike Opengraph
+unfurling): bots of a self-hosted Campfire often live on the same box or LAN, and
+only the bot's creator or an administrator can set the URL.
+
+Bots are room members, so they already appear in the room's @-mention autocomplete
+(`Autocompletable::UsersController` lists `room.users.active`).
+
+### Triggering
+
+`Room#receive` (after commit of every new message) calls `deliver_webhooks_later`:
+for each mentioned (`Message#mentionees`) active bot with a webhook it enqueues
+`User::DeliverWebhookJob`. The HTTP call never happens in the poster's request.
+
+Messages authored by bots never trigger webhooks — webhook HTML payloads contain
+mention sgids, so two bots could otherwise ping-pong forever.
+
+### Delivery: `User::Bot::Webhook`
+
+`bot.webhook` returns a `User::Bot::Webhook` (nil if no URL / not an active bot).
+`#deliver(message, event: "mention")` POSTs JSON (`Content-Type: application/json`):
+
+    { event: "mention",
+      author:  { id, name },
+      room:    { id, name, reply_to: room_bot_messages_path(room, bot.bot_key) },
+      message: { id, body: { text, html }, link: room_at_message_path(room, message) } }
+
+- `body.text` is `plain_text_body` with the bot's own `@Name` (and a trailing `:`/`,`)
+  removed and whitespace tidied; `body.html` is the raw rich-text content
+  (`message.body.body.to_html`), including `<action-text-attachment>` mentions.
+- Paths are relative; `reply_to` embeds the bot code so the bot can post later.
+- `event` is a keyword argument so new event types can reuse the delivery;
+  `#deliver_joined(room)` sends the `joined` payload through the same `deliver_to`.
+
+Response handling:
+
+- `200` + `text/plain` → body posted as the bot (escaped, newlines → `<br>`, same as
+  plain-text bot posts). `200` + `text/html` → body posted as rich text (Action Text
+  sanitizes on render). Empty bodies or bodies over 1 MB post nothing.
+- `204`, any other status or content type → nothing posted.
+- Timeouts (7s open/read/write) post "Failed to respond within 7 seconds" as the bot,
+  so people see why there was no answer. Connection errors are logged only.
+- Replies are posted only if the bot is still a member of the room, and are
+  broadcast like any message. Redirects aren't followed. Jobs aren't retried (a
+  retry could make the bot act twice).
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators set **"Bots per member"** on `/account/edit`
+(`Account#settings.bots_per_member`, a `has_json` integer, default `5`; the model
+validates it's a whole number `>= 0`, and `AccountsController#update` redirects back
+with the error as a flash alert instead of raising).
+
+- `User#bot_limit` is that setting, or `nil` for administrators (not limited).
+- `User#reached_bot_limit?` counts `bots.undeleted_bots`: everything but
+  `deactivated` (deleted) bots. Banned bots still count.
+- Enforced twice: `BotsController` answers `403` to `new`/`create` at the limit, and
+  a `on: :create` validation on bots (`creator_must_be_under_bot_limit`) refuses any
+  other path (console, future endpoints). It runs inside `create!`'s transaction,
+  which is `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` on SQLite (`default_transaction_mode: immediate`), so
+  two concurrent creates can't both slip under the limit.
+- At the limit, `/bots` hides "Add a chat bot" and shows a `#bot_limit` note that
+  the member has "reached the limit".
+- Lowering the limit never touches existing bots — the validation is create-only,
+  so they can still be edited, and deleting one frees a slot.
+
+## Misbehaving bots: flooding, failing webhooks, suspension
+
+`User::Bot::Suspendable` (included by `User::Bot`). The limits are constants in that
+module, deliberately not account settings:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `FLOOD_LIMIT` / `FLOOD_PERIOD` | 10 posts in any 10 seconds (sliding window) |
+| `FLOOD_COOLDOWN` | 30 seconds |
+| `FLOOD_COOLDOWN_ATTEMPTS` | 5 attempts during one cooldown → suspended (`flooding`) |
+| `WEBHOOK_FAILURE_LIMIT` | 10 failed deliveries in a row → suspended (`failing_webhook`) |
+
+Columns on `users`: `recent_post_times` (JSON array of epoch seconds of the last ≤10
+accepted posts), `flood_cooldown_until`, `flood_cooldown_attempts`,
+`webhook_failures_count`, `suspended_at`, `suspension_reason`. They're written with
+`update_columns` so a bot's `updated_at` (used in avatar URLs) doesn't churn on every post.
+
+### Flooding
+
+Every bot post goes through `bot.post_within_limits { ... }`: the API
+(`Messages::ByBotsController`) and webhook replies (`Webhook#post_reply_to`). It runs
+the bookkeeping in a `lock!`ed transaction (`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` on SQLite, so concurrent
+posts serialize), commits it, then yields or raises:
+
+- `Suspendable::Suspended` → API `403`; webhook reply dropped.
+- `Suspendable::CoolingDown#retry_after` → API `429` + `Retry-After: <seconds left, ceil>`;
+  webhook reply dropped.
+
+The post that goes over the limit is rejected and starts the cooldown (attempts reset
+to 0). Each further attempt during that cooldown (API or webhook reply) increments
+the attempts; the 5th suspends the bot and gets `403`. Only valid posts are checked:
+`404` (not a member) and `422` (empty body) happen before throttling.
+
+The "Failed to respond within 7 seconds" timeout notice isn't the bot's own post, so
+it doesn't count towards the limit (otherwise a slow webhook would end up suspended
+for "flooding" instead of for failing); it's just not posted while the bot is cooling
+down or suspended.
+
+### Failing webhooks
+
+`Webhook#deliver_to` calls `bot.record_webhook_delivery(succeeded:)`: success is any
+`2xx` (`Net::HTTPSuccess`), regardless of whether a reply is posted; non-`2xx`,
+timeouts and connection errors are failures. A success resets the counter.
+
+### Suspended bots
+
+`suspended?` is `suspended_at.present?`; the user's `status` stays `active`, so the
+bot keeps its memberships, messages, mentions and its slot in its creator's bot limit,
+and stays in `manageable_bots`. `authenticate_bot` still finds it (so the API can
+answer `403` rather than `401`). `bot.webhook` is `nil` while suspended, which stops
+every webhook (mention and `joined`, including jobs enqueued before the suspension).
+
+`/bots` shows "Suspended" and `suspension_reason_description`. Administrators get a
+"Reactivate" button (`POST /bots/:bot_id/reactivation`,
+`Bots::ReactivationsController`, `ensure_can_administer`; native confirm via
+`data-turbo-confirm`). `User#reactivate` clears the suspension and all counters.
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..677471c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_path.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# Bots authenticate with their bot key in the URL (POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages),
+# so the key would end up in the "Started POST ..." log line and in instrumentation
+# payloads. Mask it there, the same way filter_parameters masks it in the params.
+module FilteredBotKeyPath
+  BOT_KEY_IN_PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/?]+/)[^/?]+(?=/messages(?:\.\w+)?(?:\?|\z))}
+
+  def filtered_path
+    super.sub(BOT_KEY_IN_PATH, '\1[FILTERED]')
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend FilteredBotKeyPath
